title: Hannah continues speaking...
new-page: true

...Her voice was sort of a commentary. “Zörgiebel’s a fool, but he’s a vain fool. He won’t want his nice new suit ruined by a riot he started. That’s what I told Peter. The whole show’s for the papers.” You sigh, you nod, you agree, half listening, your eyes were moving. You couldn’t shake that, really, your Wife had made sure of that.

You noticed, in the twinkle of your little eye, at the Kösliner Strasse, a cluster of police in their long blue coats. They were standing rigid, shakos pulled low, the shiny peak of Pickelhaube amongst them. You spat at the idea, that was the ghost of an era, a war you thankfully just narrowly escaped. Had you been born four years earlier, though? You’d have been killed. 

You’d have been murdered on the fields of France, your body would’ve been cast out by a artillery shell, nothing of you would remain, your mother would get a telegram home, but nothing could bring you back...

You had always had a sort of guilt about that. About, not fighting, it was foolish, you had always kind of been a socialist, but you remember the war fever back then. But then you also remember it wasn’t so simple, you were in Berlin when you fourteen, your father, or was it your uncle? 

Was at a demonstration against the war, before the SPD went for it, there must have been…A hundred thousand? No, less, but, a lot, you were all against the war, none of you were embraced in the mad fever of war! 

Your brothers, they weren’t as lucky as you to have been born in the Millennia. You tried not to think about them, their fates, you were just grateful it wasn’t you. But..You couldn’t really shake it, why’d they have to die? 

You bit your lip, it wasn’t just a supernatural incident that took your brothers away, no, that would be too easy, you knew what it was, complictly! You tried to shake it from your mind. This was ruining your day, your eyes darted for the first thing they saw, it was Erika. Her blue eyes, God, she was beautiful, but her face wasn’t open as much anymore. 

“Been there since this morning,” Said she, voice lower than normal,  “Look at them.” She insisted, so you looked, begrudgingly, you noticed the flatbed of a lorry, idling behind them. There was a tarp, you knew that probably didn’t hide the Police’s vegetables, rather the long, black snout of a machine gun was probably beneath it. 

“Yes, look at them,” You said, biting your tongue, “But don’t mind them, Erika, I’m here.” You said, playfully, trying to ease the tension. “What would they do? Shoot into us?” She squeezed your hand, a gentle, confident pressure, “Don’t even joke, Karl.” You raised your hands playfully “Yes Ma’am, at your orders.”

